+# The Daily Grind
+
+A static Vite homepage with a browser-only encrypted delivery-note preview.
+
+- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` installs pinned dependencies.
+- `pnpm build` produces the static website in `dist/`.
+- `pnpm test` runs cryptographic round-trip and tamper-rejection checks without a server.
+
+Serve production assets from `dist/` over HTTPS. The start script is configured for the development environment; no server is required for building or running tests.
+
+## Encryption boundary
+
+Hybrid mode uses the noble library's X-Wing construction (ML-KEM-768 + X25519). Standalone post-quantum mode uses ML-KEM-768. Classical mode uses Web Crypto ECDH P-256. The bundled noble implementation provides post-quantum support without depending on experimental native KEM APIs or loading cryptography from a runtime CDN.
+
+Each note uses a new encapsulation or ephemeral ECDH key pair, HKDF-SHA-256 with a random 32-byte salt and protocol-specific context, and AES-256-GCM with a random 12-byte IV and 128-bit tag. Version, suite, recipient public-key fingerprint, record ID, and creation time are authenticated. The JSON envelope contains only public metadata, the capsule, salt, IV, and ciphertext including its tag. The note itself is never put into URLs, browser storage, analytics, or network requests.
+
+This is explicitly a local preview, not a live delivery integration. Recipient keys exist in memory in the current tab. No private keys are downloaded or persisted. Refreshing or leaving the page destroys access to encrypted downloads. Revealed notes are hidden when the tab becomes hidden. Clear removes the encrypted result and zeroes library private-key arrays; JavaScript cannot guarantee complete erasure of strings or garbage-collected copies.
+
+For actual delivery, replace the preview recipient generation with an authenticated, pinned delivery-team public key; send only encrypted envelopes to an authorized order API; and perform decryption only in the recipient's trusted environment. Do not distribute the recipient private key to customers. HTTPS, protection against malicious scripts, recipient key authentication, authorization, replay policy, and key rotation remain responsibilities of that integration. Key agreement and authenticated encryption do not authenticate the sender.
