+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage with an in-browser receipt and gift voucher signing studio. No backend or build step is required. Serve over HTTPS (or localhost) for Web Crypto access.
+
+## Signing and trust
+
+- Classical ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256 using native Web Crypto. This is not a post-quantum algorithm.
+- A non-exportable private CryptoKey is generated locally and saved with its public key in IndexedDB. Concurrent tabs reuse the first stored key. If storage is unavailable, the page reports that the key is session-only.
+- Signatures cover the version, suite, key fingerprint and every payload field using sorted JSON keys, plus a length-prefixed application context specific to receipts or vouchers.
+- Downloads contain the signed document, not a private key. Public verification keys are exported separately with a full SHA-256 fingerprint.
+- Verification defaults to this browser's public key. A different key must be explicitly imported and trusted for the current session. Verify its fingerprint with the original issuer through a trusted channel. Keys embedded in signed documents are never implicitly trusted.
+- A valid signature establishes integrity and possession of the trusted signing key. This local studio does not establish merchant authorization, payment, balance, expiration, or redemption status. Real merchant issuance requires an authenticated issuance system and independently distributed trusted merchant keys.
+- Clearing site storage loses the signing key. Export the public key so existing documents can still be verified elsewhere. Same-origin scripts have access to signing operations; the non-exportable flag prevents private-key export, not misuse by compromised scripts.
+
+## Verification
+
+Run `npm test` for cryptographic round-trip, tampering, wrong-key, malformed document, and key import checks. Browser scripts can be syntax-checked with `node --check app.mjs` and `node --check signing.mjs`.
+
+The site uses an Unsplash stock photo and Google Fonts, with local font fallbacks. No runtime JavaScript dependencies are used.
