@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+Client performance monitoring
+=============================
+
+The homepage loads `performance-monitor.js`. Navigation links, Order Now, and
+Seasonal Favorites card clicks produce `click-to-paint` samples. Synchronous
+History API updates, back/forward navigation, and hash changes also produce
+`view-switch-to-paint` samples.
+
+For asynchronous client views, wrap the update and resolve its promise after the
+final DOM commit (including any framework render completion):
+
+```js
+await window.dailyGrindPerformance.measureViewSwitch('menu', async () => {
+  const menu = await loadMenu();
+  renderMenu(menu);
+});
+```
+
+Subscribe to `window`'s `daily-grind:performance` custom event to consume samples
+from `event.detail`. Each includes `kind`, `label`, `startTime`, `duration` in
+milliseconds, and `source`. No telemetry is transmitted. The last 100 samples are
+available from `window.dailyGrindPerformance.getMeasurements()`. The latest sample
+of each kind is also a User Timing measure named `daily-grind:<kind>`.
+
+`event-timing` uses the browser's click duration through its next paint when that
+entry arrives before publication. Event Timing has browser support and duration
+threshold limitations. `paint-opportunity` is an estimate using two animation
+frames and a subsequent task; it is not proof of physical display presentation.
+These measure the next visual response after the handler or completed view update,
+not completion of animations or independently loading images. History-only
+measurements cannot infer asynchronous rendering; use the explicit hook for that.
+Overlapping history and explicit samples are separate measurements.
+
+The existing links remain full-page navigations. Measurements are discarded if
+the document hides or unloads before completion; destination-page paint is not a
+client-side update and cannot be timed from this document. Failed explicit view
+updates are also discarded.
+
+Run the non-browser checks with `node --test performance-monitor.test.cjs`.
