@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+# Equipment connections
+
+The homepage connects directly from the visitor's browser. No proxy, backend,
+discovery scan, automatic connection, or brewing command is used.
+
+Serve the homepage over HTTPS (a trustworthy localhost development origin also
+works). Use a browser supporting local/loopback network access. Each card makes
+one GET request after an explicit submit, with a 15-second timeout. Cancel,
+Disconnect, or editing the URL aborts pending requests and clears the local result.
+A successful readable 2xx response means the endpoint was reached at the displayed
+time; it is not an ongoing device session or proof of brewing readiness.
+
+Example read-only endpoints, editable in the panel:
+
+- Brewer: `http://192.168.1.100/api/status` on the shop's private LAN.
+- Helper: `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health` on the computer running the browser.
+
+Adapt the address, port, and path to your hardware/helper API. The panel accepts
+private IPv4, private IPv6, and `.local` brewer addresses, and loopback helper
+addresses. It rejects public addresses, embedded URL credentials, fragments, and
+non-HTTP schemes. Redirects are rejected. No response schema is required; response
+bodies are not displayed or retained. Requests omit credentials and referrers.
+Endpoints requiring authentication need a separate, documented pairing design.
+
+Configure each endpoint to return standard CORS headers allowing the deployed
+homepage's exact origin, e.g. `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://coffee.example`.
+Use an origin allowlist rather than reflecting arbitrary origins. Local network
+permissions do not replace CORS. The helper should bind to loopback only. Both
+servers must expose read-only health/status routes; this repository does not
+implement device firmware or the desktop companion.
+
+The independent `local-network` and `loopback-network` permissions are queried
+and observed where supported. Unsupported queries fall back to explicit-click
+requests with actionable failure messages. Fetch declares `targetAddressSpace`
+as `local` or `loopback`, respectively. No deprecated PNA preflight headers or
+legacy combined permission query are used. Browser restrictions cannot be
+bypassed; support, CORS, endpoint availability, and permissions all affect success.
+
+If embedded cross-origin, the embedding site must delegate the applicable
+`local-network` and `loopback-network` permissions through its Permissions-Policy
+and iframe allow attributes. A standalone page requires no extra delegation.
