+# Daily Grind origin transfer
+
+This static app now builds two deployment bundles. No origin has been deployed or installed apps moved by editing this repository.
+
+## Configure and build
+
+Edit `migration.config.json` before deployment:
+
+- Replace the reserved `.example` origins with the real old and new HTTPS origins. Both must share a registrable domain. The build checks the Public Suffix List, including private suffixes such as `github.io`.
+- Set `oldId` to the **existing installed app's resolved manifest ID**. If its manifest had no `id`, use its resolved `start_url` identity. Keep serving the manifest at its existing URL (copy the generated manifest there if it was not `/manifest.webmanifest`). Changing the old identity creates a different app and breaks migration.
+- Set `newId` to a stable destination app ID. This implementation assumes both apps serve their homepage and transfer assets at the origin root.
+- Set `settingsKeys` to the exact localStorage keys used for user configuration. The repository previously had no configuration storage; the supplied `daily-grind.settings` and `daily-grind.preferences` keys are explicit conventions. Do not include authentication tokens or session data.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+pnpm build
+pnpm test
+```
+
+Deploy `dist/new/` to the new origin, then `dist/old/` to the old origin. Include the hidden `.well-known` directory. Serve manifests as `application/manifest+json` and `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json`, with no login requirement or redirects. Ensure the homepage, manifest, icon, transfer page and JS files remain accessible on both origins. If an existing service worker caches these URLs, update its cache/version so the new manifest and transfer controls reach users; this repository has no service worker.
+
+Do not redirect the whole old origin or remove it during the migration window. Old installations must still fetch the manifest, association file and original settings. Retain any unrelated existing association entries if deploying into an existing `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` file.
+
+## User configuration
+
+At the new origin, **Transfer my settings** opens the old origin as a top-level window. The new app sends a request with a random nonce; both sides check exact origins and window identity. Only approved settings keys are copied, existing destination values win, and original settings are never deleted. Top-level access avoids third-party iframe storage partitioning. Failed writes roll back newly copied keys. Transfers are limited to 256 KiB.
+
+If popup policies, COOP headers, storage access restrictions or an unavailable source prevent transfer, users can download a settings backup on the old app and import it on the new app. Both origins must use a compatible opener policy for the popup flow (for example, no restrictive COOP policy); keep existing security policies and use the backup fallback when necessary. Do not put settings in URLs.
+
+Cookies, IndexedDB, notification permissions and sessions are not copied by this implementation. There are no such stores in the supplied application. If the deployed app uses them, implement server-side synchronization or a separately validated export before rollout. Users must sign in again and grant origin-scoped permissions again.
+
+## Installation and shortcuts
+
+The destination manifest declares `migrate_from`, the old manifest announces `migrate_to`, and the old origin authorizes the destination ID. Supported browsers offer the installation update and preserve OS integration. The browser controls the update prompt; page JavaScript cannot migrate an installation directly. The rollout uses suggested migration so users can copy settings before accepting the update.
+
+Automatic origin migration is limited in browser support. The old app remains usable and links to the new app. Where migration is unavailable, users transfer settings, install the new site using their browser's app menu, and recreate shortcuts manually. Automatic shortcut preservation cannot be promised on unsupported browsers.
+
+References: [Chrome origin migration](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration) and [manifest migration proposal](https://wicg.github.io/manifest-incubations/).
+
+Validation uses Node tests and static build output only. No development server or manual browser verification is required.
