+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+The app now links an install manifest. The migration build produces two static
+deployment bundles with a native PWA origin-migration handshake. The page design
+and routes are unchanged.
+
+## Configure and build
+
+Copy `migration.config.example.json` to `migration.config.json` and replace both
+example origins with the actual HTTPS origins. They must be **same-site** (share
+the same registrable domain); this is a browser requirement, not merely a common
+hostname suffix. The example domains are placeholders, not deployment targets.
+
+`oldId` must exactly match the installed app's existing resolved manifest ID.
+If the original manifest omitted `id`, use its original resolved `start_url`.
+The default `/` assumes the installed app used the origin root. Do not change
+the old identity as part of this rollout. `newId` is the destination's permanent
+identity. This static app is served at `/` on both origins.
+
+```sh
+npm run build -- --config migration.config.json
+```
+
+The build checks origin and ID syntax, manifest links, PNG dimensions, and both
+sides of the handshake. It does not validate DNS, same-site eligibility, remote
+responses, or an existing browser installation. `npm run build` without a config
+builds only `dist/current`, without enabling migration.
+
+## Deploy
+
+1. Publish **the contents** of `dist/new` at the new origin first.
+2. Publish **the contents** of `dist/old` at the old origin. If that origin has
+   an existing manifest at another URL, serve the generated old manifest there
+   too: installed clients must keep discovering updates at the original URL.
+3. Serve `manifest.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json` and
+   `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json`. Include the
+   hidden `.well-known` directory in deployment. If an association file already
+   exists, merge the generated entry into it, preserving other app entries.
+4. Keep the old page, icons, manifest, and association file available over HTTPS,
+   without authentication or redirects. Keep both origins available while users
+   accept the update. Configure revalidation for manifest/association responses;
+   existing service workers must not indefinitely serve stale manifests.
+
+The new manifest declares `migrate_from`, the old origin authorizes the exact new
+ID with `allow_migration`, and the old manifest proactively signals `migrate_to`.
+The browser offers its native update dialog. Suggested migration lets users keep
+using the old app until they accept; no scripted uninstall/reinstall is used.
+The build does not contact or publish to either origin.
+
+## What is preserved
+
+Supported Chromium browsers perform the installation transfer and carry forward
+browser-managed app configuration and OS integration, including shortcuts.
+The browser owns this process and requires the installed user's acceptance;
+website JavaScript cannot silently retarget OS shortcuts.
+
+Cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB, caches, and origin permissions are **not**
+transferred by this protocol. This repository has no app setting storage or
+account backend to migrate. If the deployed app has additional saved settings,
+restore them through its authenticated backend before retiring the old origin.
+Do not assume the handshake migrates those values or copy credentials in URLs.
+
+Chrome documents same-site origin migration starting in Chrome 150. Browsers
+that ignore these manifest members retain the old installation, so leave the old
+app available. Do not replace this process with an automatic redirect or promise
+shortcut preservation after a manual reinstall.
+
+References:
+
+- [Chrome origin migration guide](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration)
+- [Chromium migration process and OS integration](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/chrome/browser/web_applications/docs/manifest_migration_process.md)
+- [WICG explainer: data and permission limitations](https://github.com/WICG/manifest-incubations/blob/gh-pages/pwa-migration-explainer.md)
