+# The Daily Grind — My Account
+
+A responsive, dependency-free account interface. The default page opens Sign-in & security. Customers can view passkey metadata, rename labels, and confirm deletion, with accessible dialogs, notifications, last-key warnings, and an empty state.
+
+## Current setup
+
+This repository contains a static site with no authentication service or server-side credential store. It runs in an explicitly labeled **demo account** by default. Sample keys and label changes persist in browser local storage. The demo never invokes authenticator APIs or creates, changes, or removes real passkeys. “Reset demo” restores the sample records. Profile and rewards panels contain sample data and are hidden when the live adapter is enabled.
+
+## Connect an authenticated backend
+
+Set the `daily-grind-passkeys-api` meta tag in `index.html` to a same-origin path such as `/api/account/passkeys`. Render a session-bound CSRF token into the `csrf-token` meta tag. The adapter sends credentials with each request and requires a CSRF token for mutations. No secrets belong in either tag. The account page must itself be served only to signed-in customers in production.
+
+The host application must implement these routes:
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`: return the complete current user's credential snapshot.
+- `PATCH /api/account/passkeys/:id`: accept `{ "name": "Personal laptop" }`, validate and persist a label of 1–50 characters, then return the complete snapshot.
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:id`: revoke the credential atomically and return the complete snapshot **after** the committed deletion. Do not return 204 or a filtered/paginated list.
+
+Every successful response has this shape:
+
+```json
+{
+  "authenticated": true,
+  "complete": true,
+  "rpId": "your-actual-relying-party-domain.example",
+  "userId": "BASE64URL_WEBAUTHN_USER_HANDLE",
+  "hasAlternativeSignIn": true,
+  "passkeys": [
+    {
+      "id": "opaque-record-id",
+      "credentialId": "BASE64URL_CREDENTIAL_ID",
+      "name": "Personal laptop",
+      "provider": "iCloud Keychain",
+      "device": "laptop",
+      "createdAt": "2026-10-12T10:00:00Z",
+      "lastUsedAt": "2026-11-20T08:00:00Z",
+      "current": false
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+`device` supports `laptop`, `phone`, or `usb`; other values show a generic key icon. Timestamps are optional and display “Not available” when absent. Only set `current` when the server can reliably identify the credential associated with the current device/session; otherwise leave false. `userId` is the original WebAuthn user handle, not an email address or database ID. Credential IDs and user handles use base64url without padding.
+
+The server must enforce authentication, record ownership, CSRF protection, and name validation. It must prevent deletion of the last sign-in method when there is no usable alternative, regardless of client checks. It should enforce recent authentication for sensitive operations according to the host account policy. Derive `hasAlternativeSignIn` from actual usable recovery or login methods. Return 401 for expired sessions, 403 for failed authorization/CSRF, and 409 for lockout prevention or stale state. Return `Cache-Control: no-store` for credential responses. Connect the header, sign-out route, and surrounding account pages to the host application's existing routes.
+
+## Browser authenticator sync
+
+After a successful live list load or confirmed deletion, `syncAuthenticator` sends the **full server-authoritative list** to `PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials` in a secure context when available. Calls are serialized to avoid out-of-order updates. Invalid/incomplete responses, failed server mutations, and demo data never reach the authenticator. An empty list is sent only when an authenticated complete snapshot explicitly confirms no remaining credentials.
+
+The browser/provider controls how it applies the signal. A resolved call is presented as “browser notified,” not guaranteed physical deletion. Unsupported APIs or provider errors show manual password-manager cleanup guidance without undoing a successful server deletion. Renaming changes the site's friendly label only: the Signal API has no method for setting an individual passkey nickname. `signalCurrentUserDetails` updates account identity metadata, so it is deliberately not called for nickname edits.
+
+Reference: [Chrome's WebAuthn Signal API documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/webauthn-signal-api).
+
+## Checks
+
+Run `npm test` for repository, mutation, and authenticator-sync unit tests. No build step is needed. The implementation was checked without starting a server, importing server entrypoints, or using a browser.
+
+Fonts are loaded from Google Fonts (DM Sans and Libre Caslon Display). The café photograph is served by Unsplash. System font fallbacks and a solid image background keep the page usable if these resources are unavailable.
