+# The Daily Grind — passkey accounts
+
+Install with `pnpm install` and build the browser asset with `pnpm build`. `pnpm start` runs the account API and homepage together. Requires Node 22.13+ (uses built-in SQLite); Node 24 LTS is recommended.
+
+For deployment set `APP_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.example` to the exact public origin, `RP_ID=your-domain.example` to its passkey domain, `NODE_ENV=production`, and `PORT` as needed. The default local origin is `http://localhost:11222`. WebAuthn requires HTTPS outside localhost. Do not change the RP ID after creating passkeys. Forward the browser's Origin header unchanged through any reverse proxy.
+
+Profiles and verified credential public keys persist under `DATA_DIR` (defaults to `./data`). Mount a durable, private volume for this directory. No passwords, biometric data, or private keys are stored by the server. Email addresses identify accounts and are **not verified**; this implementation does not send email or provide email-based recovery. Users should add a second passkey, and the final credential cannot be removed.
+
+The native browser WebAuthn API handles discoverable sign-in and registration. SimpleWebAuthn verifies challenges, origin, RP ID, signatures, and user verification on the server. Challenges expire after five minutes and are single-use. Profile changes, adding a passkey, and removing a passkey require reauthentication scoped to the current account. Reauthentication expires after two minutes and is consumed by profile changes and removals. Session IDs rotate on sign-in; cookies are HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, and Secure on HTTPS.
+
+Sessions and rate limits are held in process memory. Restarting signs everyone out. This is a single-process implementation; use a shared session/rate-limit store before running multiple instances. Back up the SQLite file using SQLite-aware backup tooling. Password-manager Signal APIs are optional enhancements; failures do not block successful account operations.
+
+Build-only validation was used. No server or browser was started during implementation.
+
+Provider names are sourced from the [passkey developer AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids). The local compact registry uses generated monogram icons rather than large embedded provider logos. Provider identification is a display hint only and does not affect authentication.
