+# The Daily Grind
+
+The homepage’s **My Account** link opens an accessible account dialog. Customers can register using a discoverable passkey, sign in without a username or password, update their name and contact email, and add, rename, or remove saved passkeys. A fresh passkey verification is required for every profile or passkey change. The last passkey cannot be removed.
+
+## Build and run
+
+Requires Node.js 24 or newer and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+pnpm run build
+pnpm start
+```
+
+The app defaults to `http://localhost:11222`. Use that exact hostname when accessing it locally. The build bundles the browser’s SimpleWebAuthn client locally; no runtime CDN is required. `build` also checks server syntax without starting or importing the server.
+
+Production configuration:
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, for example `https://coffee.example.com` (no trailing slash). This determines the allowed WebAuthn origin, relying-party hostname, and secure-cookie policy. HTTPS termination must forward `/api/*` to this Node app.
+- `PORT`: listening port, default `11222`.
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: persistent SQLite file, default `data/accounts.sqlite`. Mount a writable persistent volume and back it up, including SQLite WAL files or using SQLite’s backup facilities. Account state must survive restarts.
+
+Serve the application with this Node backend, rather than a static file server, for account features. A static-only deployment displays an account-service error and cannot register or authenticate customers. Deploy a single application instance with its SQLite database; use shared database storage and a shared rate limiter before scaling to multiple instances.
+
+## Authentication behavior
+
+- SimpleWebAuthn verifies registration attestations and authentication signatures server-side with mandatory user verification, expected origin, RP ID, and single-use, session-bound challenges expiring after five minutes.
+- The database stores public keys, counters, transport hints, backup status, names, and use dates. Private keys and biometric data remain with the authenticator.
+- Opaque session tokens are hashed in the database, expire after 12 hours, and rotate on registration/sign-in. Cookies are HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax, with Secure and the `__Host-` prefix on HTTPS. Signing out revokes the server-side session.
+- State-changing API requests require an exact matching Origin. Responses are not cached. A per-address request limit provides basic abuse protection; configure additional limits at your trusted production ingress if needed.
+- Reauthentication grants expire after two minutes, authorize only the requested action (and credential ID for rename/removal), and are consumed once. Removing a passkey signs out other sessions. At most 10 passkeys can be saved per account.
+- Email addresses are unique contact identifiers, **not verified email ownership**. Updating an email does not change the passkey user handle or rename credentials in an external password manager. The UI makes clear that sign-in uses the passkey.
+- There is no password fallback or email recovery. Customers should add a backup passkey or use a synced password manager; losing every passkey means losing access. Deleting a passkey here revokes it on the server but does not remove its copy from a password manager.
+
+Passkey API reference: [SimpleWebAuthn server documentation](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server/) and [browser documentation](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/browser/).
+
+## Verification
+
+`pnpm run build` performs server syntax checks and builds the browser bundle. No development server, preview server, or manual browser verification was used during implementation. End-to-end authenticator ceremonies require a supported browser on HTTPS or localhost and have not been exercised here.
