 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Passkey UI and authentication service
+
+The sign-in and sign-up pages use native WebAuthn device prompts. Account settings
+are at `/devtools-times/account/settings` and support adding, renaming, and removing
+passkeys, with an identity-verification dialog when the server requires a fresh
+session. Password authentication remains available. Browser support and HTTPS
+are required for passkeys (localhost is also a secure context).
+
+The repository is a frontend for an external authentication service. Set
+`PUBLIC_AUTH_API_URL` at build time to change its API base. The existing default is
+`https://us-central1-web-devrel-apps.cloudfunctions.net/times_cdn/api`.
+**The service must implement the following contract before passkeys can work end
+to end.** The UI does not create fake sessions or store credentials locally.
+
+| Method | Endpoint | Request / response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| POST | `/login` | `{ username, password }`; establishes session |
+| POST | `/register` | `{ username, password }`; creates password account |
+| GET | `/session` | `{ user: { username, hasPassword } }`, or `{ user: null }` |
+| POST | `/logout` | Ends session |
+| GET | `/passkeys` | `{ passkeys: [{ id, name, createdAt, lastUsedAt? }] }` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/register/options` | Optional `{ username }`; returns `{ ceremonyId, options }` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/register/verify` | `{ ceremonyId, credential, name }`; verifies attestation and saves credential |
+| POST | `/passkeys/authenticate/options` | `{ purpose: "signin" \| "reauth", username? }`; returns `{ ceremonyId, options }` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/authenticate/verify` | `{ ceremonyId, credential, purpose }`; verifies assertion and establishes or refreshes session |
+| POST | `/reauthenticate` | `{ password }`; refreshes current session’s verification |
+| PATCH | `/passkeys/:id` | `{ name }`; renames an owned credential |
+| DELETE | `/passkeys/:id` | Removes an owned credential |
+
+`options` follow the WebAuthn JSON format with base64url-encoded buffers. Passkey
+sign-up supplies a username; settings registration uses the signed-in user. A
+successful passkey sign-up establishes a session. Successful endpoints return JSON
+(or 204 for mutations); failed verification must return a non-2xx status. A 403
+on a management operation opens reauthentication, then the user confirms the
+original operation again. A 401 indicates an expired session.
+
+The service must generate and verify single-use, expiring challenges, bind each
+ceremony to its intended user and purpose, verify RP ID, allowed origin and user
+verification, enforce ownership and recent verification for mutations, and reject
+removal of the final sign-in method. Create new accounts only after successful
+registration verification. Use a WebAuthn verification library on the server.
+Sessions must use secure HttpOnly cookies; cross-origin hosting requires an
+explicit credentialed CORS allowlist, appropriate cookie settings, and server
+origin/CSRF validation. The UI never treats the device prompt alone as successful
+authentication. Biometric data stays with the authenticator.
+
+Run `node --test tests/auth.test.mjs` (Node 24+) for the WebAuthn and service adapter
+checks, and `pnpm build` to check the production build. Tests mock the authenticator
+and service; they do not start a server or replace device and backend integration
+testing. The buffer conversion follows the
+[WebAuthn JSON representation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PublicKeyCredential/parseCreationOptionsFromJSON_static).
