@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@
+# Privacy and deployment review
+
+## Current data inventory
+
+The published site is static HTML, one stylesheet, and one JPEG. There is no
+client-side JavaScript, analytics, advertising, fingerprinting, permission
+request, form submission, cookie, Web Storage, IndexedDB, or service worker.
+The CSP explicitly disables scripts, connections, forms, embeds, and workers.
+No personal data is collected or retained by application code. Account, rewards,
+and ordering routes remain placeholders; the misleading logout link was removed
+because there is no session or logout implementation.
+
+The host necessarily receives IP addresses and request metadata. Before production,
+configure the host/CDN to omit query strings, cookies, authorization values, and
+request bodies from logs, restrict log access, and set the shortest operationally
+necessary retention period. Confirm those settings and the provider's terms with
+the operator; they cannot be enforced by this static repository. Update the public
+privacy notice with any established hosting retention period.
+
+## Third-party audit
+
+| Component | Finding and action | Visitor data disclosure |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash hero photo | Previously fetched on each visit. Downloaded the existing photo into `assets/coffee.jpg`; no visual replacement. | No request to Unsplash from the published page. |
+| Fonts and CSS | System fonts; extracted inline CSS into a local stylesheet. | Only the site host receives asset requests. |
+| Scripts, SDKs, embeds | None present; CSP denies them. | None. |
+| Runtime packages | No production dependencies. | None. |
+| Hosting/CDN | Deployment provider is not configured in an account by this change. Netlify configuration is supplied. | IP and request metadata; operator must confirm logging and retention. |
+| Development tools | The existing `start` command downloads `statikk`; guidance tooling was used during development. Neither is included in the publish directory. | No visitor integration. |
+
+Original photo source (development-time download only):
+https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80
+
+Run `npm run audit:privacy` when changing HTML, CSS, assets, or dependencies; it also
+runs automatically before each build. This is a source audit, not a substitute for
+a production network audit. No browser or network-traffic verification was performed
+for this change. Before introducing any service, and at each release that changes
+an integration, record its purpose, requested fields, recipients, retention,
+subprocessors, and deletion mechanism. Reject speculative data collection. Review
+the inventory quarterly. Once authorized to inspect production traffic, compare a
+redacted network capture against this inventory without retaining visitor data.
+
+## Security headers and publishing
+
+Run `npm run build` and publish **only `dist/`**. `netlify.toml` selects that build
+and directory. On Netlify, the copied `_headers` file applies response headers to
+all routes. Other hosts must configure equivalent HTTP headers; the existing
+generic development server does not guarantee support for `_headers`. Check the
+deployed response headers through the hosting provider before declaring them live.
+
+The early HTML CSP and referrer meta tags provide a fallback, but meta tags cannot
+enforce `frame-ancestors`, Permissions Policy, HSTS, or the other HTTP-only headers.
+Do not replace the response headers with meta tags. CSS and the photo use same-origin
+paths; serve CSS as `text/css`, JPEG as `image/jpeg`, and HTML as `text/html` so
+`nosniff` can operate correctly.
+
+HSTS starts at 300 seconds for a safe HTTPS rollout, without committing other
+subdomains or preloading. Raise it after confirming the production TLS setup.
+COOP permits future outbound popup flows. Cross-origin isolation is not enabled
+because the site requires no shared-memory features. No reporting endpoint is
+configured: there is no backend to receive and redact reports, and adding external
+telemetry would introduce a new data recipient.
+
+## Requirements before adding personal data flows
+
+- Ask only for data needed for the immediate action, with an explanation next to
+  each field. Offer guest ordering and keep marketing consent optional and separate.
+- Validate requests and authorization on the server. Use HTTPS, CSRF protection for
+  state changes, origin checks, and rate limits on sensitive endpoints. Never put
+  personal data, tokens, or session identifiers into URLs, analytics, or logs.
+- Keep authentication server-side. Use a host-only cookie such as
+  `__Host-session` with `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/` and no `Domain`.
+  Never store credentials or personal data in localStorage or sessionStorage.
+- Implement logout as a CSRF-protected POST that invalidates the server session,
+  expires the session cookie, and returns `Cache-Control: no-store` and
+  `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`. Do not put Clear-Site-Data on
+  ordinary page responses. Sensitive account and order responses must also use
+  `Cache-Control: no-store`; client cleanup cannot invalidate a server session.
+- Define enforced retention/deletion jobs and usable access, correction, export,
+  and deletion flows before collection. Do not reuse order data for marketing.
+- Reassess CSP and permissions narrowly for each feature. Keep executable code
+  first-party where possible; review third parties before loading them. Prefer
+  user-activated embed facades with local thumbnails. Pin and integrity-check any
+  approved immutable external scripts/styles and omit credentials and referrers.
+  Never weaken the existing policies globally just to accommodate an SDK.
