--- /dev/null
+++ b/AUTHENTICATION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Homepage sign-in integration
+
+The static homepage implements in-place browser credential requests and an inline
+password form. It does not include an identity database or authentication server.
+Deploy over HTTPS and implement these same-origin endpoints before enabling real
+sign-ins. A credential chooser result alone never signs a customer in.
+
+All endpoints accept JSON via POST, return non-2xx on failure, and must use
+`Cache-Control: no-store`. Successful verification must establish an authenticated
+session using a Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookie. Return JSON (or 204) on successful
+verification, never a redirect to a login page.
+
+- `/api/login/options`: return WebAuthn request options directly as JSON, including
+  a cryptographically random, base64url challenge and the configured `rpId`.
+  Omit `allowCredentials` or use an empty array. Use `userVerification: "preferred"`.
+  Bind the challenge and verification policy to the visitor's server session. Allow
+  a prepared challenge to remain valid for at least 60 seconds.
+- `/api/login/verify`: receive the assertion produced by `credential.toJSON()`.
+  Use a vetted WebAuthn server library to verify the single-use session challenge,
+  expected origin, RP ID, signature, credential ownership and stored verification
+  policy. Return 404 only for an unknown credential ID; the homepage uses that
+  response to signal a stale passkey to supporting password managers.
+- `/api/login/password`: receive `{ "username": "…", "password": "…" }`; verify
+  against the account store with secure password hashing and establish the session.
+  Apply rate limiting and generic failure messages.
+
+Enforce same-origin/CSRF protections on every endpoint (including password login),
+expire and consume challenges, rotate session IDs after authentication, and never
+log passwords. `/logout` and `/forgot-password` remain the site's existing routes.
+Rewards, My Account and Order Now retain their original destinations; after server
+verification, the selected destination is resumed. Header Sign in completes in place.
+Cancelling leaves the customer on the homepage. Modifier-click links keep native
+browser behavior. No credentials or client-side authentication flags are persisted.
+
