index 0000000..6961b30
--- /dev/null
+++ b/SECURITY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+## Audit findings and fixes
+
+- The original Node server returned the home page for every file request, sent no security headers, and treated logout as a text response. `server.js` now starts a small dependency-free Node HTTP server, while `app.js` owns the request handler. `npm start` and the existing PORT default (11222) remain supported. Only explicit page routes and `/styles.css` are served; private project files and unknown routes return 404. Existing page links still show the same home page, as before.
+- The HTML contained a style block and one style attribute. Both moved to `styles.css`, preserving the appearance while allowing CSP to reject inline styles. The only external resource is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background photograph. No third-party executable resources, forms, embeds, popups, client scripts, DOM injection sinks, messaging, WebSockets or browser storage code were found. No unnecessary client script or Trusted Types policy was added.
+- CSP is enforced immediately because the complete, small source was audited: deny resources by default, prohibit scripts and inline handlers, permit local CSS and only the existing image origin, deny objects, base tags and framing, constrain forms to this origin, and enforce Trusted Types with no allowed policy creation. Browsers without Trusted Types support retain CSP protection. If scripts are added, authorize only the intended script with a hash or per-response cryptographic nonce and strict-dynamic after auditing it; do not loosen the policy to unsafe-inline or unsafe-eval.
+- Responses include nosniff, no-referrer, DENY framing, restricted feature permissions, same-origin CORP, and COOP same-origin-allow-popups. No CORS grants are needed. COEP/cross-origin isolation was deliberately omitted: there is no shared-memory feature requirement, and it would add third-party resource compatibility risk.
+- Fetch Metadata checks reject foreign subresource and report requests, including requests from sibling subdomains, while permitting incoming document links. Vary covers all Fetch Metadata inputs. Legacy clients lacking these headers retain access. These checks supplement CSP and are not authentication.
+
+## Logout and session scope
+
+`GET /logout` and `/logout/` send `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`, no-store, root-path cookie expiration headers and a static confirmation page. HEAD performs no logout. Known cross-site and same-site requests and prefetch requests cannot log out a visitor. No redirect target or cookie value is reflected into the page.
+
+There is no login system, token verifier, identity provider or server-side session store in this repository. Logout clears browser-held state; there is no server session to revoke. If authentication is introduced, invalidate its session/refresh token at the provider or store before returning this response, use a CSRF-protected POST flow, and issue host-only `__Host-` cookies with Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax and Path=/.
+
+Clear-Site-Data requires HTTPS and browser support. Its cookies directive also clears cookies on the registrable domain's subdomains, so deploy this site on a dedicated domain if sibling apps must retain their cookies. Explicit expiration is a fallback for root-path, host-only cookies received by this application; it cannot remove arbitrary Domain/Path variants or all storage in unsupported browsers. See [MDN Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data).
+
+## Deployment and safe policy changes
+
+1. Use Node 22 or later and terminate HTTPS at your deployment proxy. Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public HTTPS origin, for example `https://coffee.example` (no trailing slash). Run with `npm start`; PORT is optional. Do not expose the internal HTTP listener directly to the public internet.
+2. PUBLIC_ORIGIN enables an absolute Reporting-Endpoints destination and report-to, plus HSTS with an initial max-age of 300 seconds. No Host or Forwarded header is trusted to build this URL. Local HTTP keeps legacy report-uri reporting and omits HSTS. Increase HSTS lifetime only after validating deployment-wide HTTPS; subdomain/preload commitments are deliberately absent.
+3. `/security-reports` accepts legacy CSP reports and Reporting API batches, caps payloads at 16 KiB and 50 reports, and logs only allowlisted directive counts. URLs, queries, source samples, cookies and identifiers are discarded. Treat these unauthenticated counts as untrusted diagnostics, not proof of an attack. Apply ingress request-rate limits and do not log report bodies at the proxy. [Reporting API guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/web-apis/reporting-api) requires an absolute reporting endpoint URL.
+4. Existing enforced CSP remains active. For future stricter policies or new dependencies, first inventory the change, add a separate Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header pointing to the same reporting endpoint, and observe representative traffic for days to weeks. Filter browser-extension noise and investigate recurring app violations before promotion. Keep reporting on enforced policies to catch regressions. Never enable COEP without a demonstrated isolation requirement and a subresource compatibility audit.
+5. Run `npm run build` before deployment. It checks syntax and exercises the handler with in-memory requests: headers, allowed resources, logout, foreign requests, file disclosure, method handling, bounded reporting and sensitive-data exclusion. It starts no listener and never imports the server entrypoint. No manual browser or live-server verification was performed for this change.
