 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## SMS verification for sign-up
+
+The sign-up form uses the same-origin `api/signup` route to send and check SMS
+codes with Twilio Verify before calling the existing registration service. Set
+these **server-only** environment variables (do not prefix them with `PUBLIC_`):
+
+- `TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID`
+- `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN`
+- `TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID`
+- `TWILIO_VERIFY_TEMPLATE_SID`
+
+Configure the Verify service for **six-digit codes** and enable Fraud Guard and
+service rate limits with the exact unique names `signup_phone` and `signup_ip`,
+with appropriate buckets for phone numbers and source IPs. The send request passes
+these keys through Twilio’s `RateLimits` parameter. The route also applies a
+30-second per-session send cooldown, a ten-minute verification lifetime, and five
+check attempts. Session cooldowns alone do not protect against new-session abuse.
+Use a shared Astro session driver for deployments with multiple server instances.
+
+The SMS template must be an approved SMS-capable Twilio custom template bound to
+the actual signup hostname. It must render human-readable text followed by a final
+line containing only `@<hostname> #<code>`, with the same generated code in both
+places. For example (replace the hostname with your deployed site's hostname):
+
+```text
+Your DevTools Times verification code is 012345.
+
+@example.com #012345
+```
+
+Do not include a scheme, port, path, or trailing signature in that final line.
+See [Twilio verification templates](https://www.twilio.com/docs/verify/verification-templates).
+Serve the signup page over HTTPS for automatic WebOTP extraction. Browsers without
+WebOTP still support keyboard autofill where available and manual code entry.
+Missing provider configuration returns an explicit unavailable error; there is no
+mock code or verification bypass.
+
+The external registration API remains outside this repository. This app gates its
+own registration route on verification and forwards `phoneNumber`; enforcing
+verification on direct calls to the external API and persisting the verified
+number there require corresponding changes in that service.
