+        <p>Our sourcing process begins with a simple question: where did this coffee come from, and whose work made it possible? Before we think about a roast profile, we look for a clear connection to the people growing and processing the beans. We work with producers, cooperatives, and sourcing partners who can tell us about a coffee’s origin, harvest, and journey. That context matters as much as the tasting notes, because a memorable cup begins with careful work at the farm.</p>
+
+        <p>We approach each harvest with curiosity rather than a fixed shopping list. Altitude, soil, shade, and rainfall all influence how a coffee develops, while picking and processing bring another layer of character. A washed lot might offer a clean, bright cup; a naturally processed coffee might bring a fuller sweetness and fruit-forward aroma. We ask our partners about these choices so we can understand what we taste and choose coffees that reflect the place and season they come from.</p>
+
+        <p>Samples then come to our roasting table. We roast small batches and taste them side by side, paying attention to sweetness, acidity, body, and balance. We return to each sample as it cools, when different qualities become easier to notice. A coffee needs more than an exciting first sip: we want it to feel satisfying through a whole cup, whether you enjoy it black, pull it as espresso, or add a splash of milk. Only after those conversations and tastings do we decide which lots belong on our menu.</p>
+
+        <p>Responsible sourcing also means looking beyond flavor. We seek partners who are willing to discuss how coffee is priced, how growers are paid, and how farms care for their land and water. We value lasting relationships that give producers a reason to plan for future harvests. There is always more to learn, and we prefer specific questions and honest conversations to broad promises. As our sourcing evolves, our aim stays the same: to make thoughtful buying decisions that recognize the skill and effort behind every bag.</p>
+
+        <p>Once the green coffee arrives, we develop a roast that brings out its individual character rather than making every origin taste alike. Small test roasts help us find the right balance, and regular tastings keep us attentive as the beans rest and age. The final step happens with you: brewing, sharing, and enjoying the result. Our menu changes with the harvest because coffee is seasonal, but our care for the journey from bean to cup remains constant.</p>
