@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+Node 24+ app, with a native HTML rewards registration form and `POST /api/verify-email`. Install dependencies with `pnpm install` and start in your deployment environment with `pnpm start`. The previous static-only server cannot handle verification; the start script now uses the Node server. No build output is needed. `pnpm build` checks syntax; `pnpm test` runs isolated cryptographic and storage tests without starting or importing the server entrypoint.
+
+## Deployment configuration
+
+- `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin (for example `https://coffee.example.com`). Required in production; never inferred from request headers. It must match the origin that serves the form, including the port where applicable.
+- `NODE_ENV=production`: enforces HTTPS configuration and Secure session cookies.
+- `ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: a valid Chrome Email Verification origin-trial token registered for your public origin. The server serves it in the `Origin-Trial` header. Register/renew through the [Chrome verifier documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier). Without a valid trial configuration, browsers may submit an empty token. No token or deployment origin has been supplied in this repository.
+- `PORT`: listening port (default 11222).
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: SQLite file path (default `./rewards.sqlite`). Use a persistent, access-restricted volume. All processes serving this origin must share this database for challenge consumption; use a shared transactional datastore if deploying across multiple hosts.
+
+Serve behind HTTPS. The browser must support the experimental API, be signed in to a participating provider, and have the relevant browser feature enabled. Gmail/Googlemail delegation to `accounts.google.com` is supported by the current verifier. Its discovery and key endpoints are explicitly allowlisted to prevent arbitrary server network access. Adding providers requires reviewing and extending the `providers` map in `lib/verify-email.mjs` with their exact HTTPS issuer and JWKS URLs; DNS delegation is still required for each submitted email domain.
+
+## Verification behavior
+
+The homepage issues a fresh random 256-bit nonce in a ten-minute SQLite challenge, associated with an opaque HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax session cookie (Secure in HTTPS). The nonce is rendered into the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input inside the form. Native form submission lets the browser populate the presentation before POST; JavaScript does not intercept it. Responses are `no-store` and restored pages reload for a fresh challenge.
+
+The endpoint checks the request origin and consumes the challenge atomically before verification. It validates DNS delegation, provider metadata, issuer signature, email/verified claims, expiration/freshness, browser signature, audience, nonce, and the SD-JWT digest using `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`. It supports `ev+sd-jwt` and the earlier `evt+jwt` trial type, and multiple provider signing keys when `kid` is absent.
+
+Only successfully verified submissions create accounts. Passwords are stored as salted scrypt hashes. Duplicate addresses do not overwrite accounts; the response avoids revealing membership. No email delivery service is used. Unsupported browsers/providers, empty tokens, expired sessions, or invalid proofs receive an actionable error and a fresh form. They never create verified accounts or trigger an OTP/magic-link email.
+
+This implements registration and persistence, not a full loyalty ledger or sign-in/account-management system. Membership copy and legal text should be reviewed against the café's actual rewards program before launch. Public production deployments should also apply edge request throttling to the homepage and registration endpoint.
