+
+## Browser agent tools
+
+The site exposes client-side tools using [WebMCP](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/).
+Use a secure context and a browser with WebMCP enabled. Unsupported browsers retain the ordinary UI.
+
+- `open-search` and `open-reading-list` are available throughout the site. They wait for hydration and article loading, then open the corresponding form.
+- The open search form exposes `search-articles`, with a required `query` field. It returns matching article metadata while displaying the results.
+- The open reading-list form exposes `manage-reading-list`, with `action` (`view`, `add`, `remove`) and an article `slug` for changes. It uses the current browser session and returns the saved articles after updating the UI.
+- Article pages expose their matching utility: `brew-coffee` (coffee-alchemy), `get-tide-levels` (coastal-pulse), `get-plant-stats` (silent-architects), or `get-article-popularity` (super-bowl-lx, required non-empty `id`). Utilities return response status, redirect information, body data, and actionable errors.
+
+The intentional DevTools network examples remain: coffee returns a server error, tides redirects, plants prepares oversized cache cookies, and the automatic popularity request omits its identifier. Agent calls can supply an identifier to the popularity utility. Utilities register only on their article page and are removed with an AbortSignal during navigation; in-flight tool requests honor cancellation.
+
+Forms use native named controls and share their submission handlers between people and agents. Browsers without declarative WebMCP ignore the tool attributes. Article utility registration is feature-detected separately from the automatic example requests.
+
+Run the server-free registration, request, cancellation, and form-readiness checks with `node --test tests/browser-tools.test.mjs`.
